  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Zonal flow aloft pushes offshore as a weak \nupper level trough out ahead of a second and stronger upper \nlevel trough noted well to the west in the Upper Midwest pushes \nacross the Southeast late tonight into Wednesday. Associated \nweak shortwave also pushes across the Carolinas tonight into \nThurs morning as well. While there will be forcing in place, \nwith ample dry air over the Mid-Atlantic, outside of high cirrus\noverspreading ENC tonight not expecting any precip with this \nshortwave. Winds look to decouple tonight as well, so if cirrus \nremains thin enough another threat for some non impactful ground\nfog will be possible especially along the coast where the \nlightest winds will be in place. Dry weather continues across \nENC through Fri with slightly above avg temps as highs get into \nthe 70s Wed/Thurs.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Biggest change in the forecast for later this\nweek and weekend is that both precip chances and winds have \nbeen trending down for this weekends system. Deep upper level \ntrough in the Upper Mid-West will dive SE'wards into the Ohio \nRiver Valley Thurs into Fri with associated jet streak pushing \noff the east coast and strengthening during this timeframe.\nAt the same time, strong shortwave also rounds the base of this\ntrough Thurs into Fri as well promoting lift across the Eastern\nSeaboard. At the surface, low pressure in the Upper Mid-West on\nWed pushes SE'wards on Thurs while deepening and then \ntransferring its energy to a second offshore low south of Long \nIsland. This has been where the biggest change in the forecast \nhas occured as ensemble, AI, and deterministic guidance has \ntrended towards a more northern track for this surface low \nwhich as a result has trended precip chances down once again to\nsub-mentionable levels and has lowered the winds. So while the\n30-40+ mph gust risk has lowered it will still be breezy across\nENC Thurs and again on Fri with steady 10-20 mph W-NW winds and\ngusts up to 25-35 mph possible. While winds have trended \ndownward, if the strongest gusts are realized over a longer \namount of time Thurs/Fri we could still see some minor coastal \nflooding concerns across the soundside OBX mainly north of \nOregon Inlet, though confidence is low that this is more than \nnuisance water level rises given the decreasing trend in wind \nguidance.\n\nBehind the system, unseasonably cool air will flood into the \narea as Canadian high pressure system builds into the Eastern\nSeaboard this weekend behind the departing low knocking highs \ndown into the 50s for the weekend and into early next week and \noffer the first real opportunity for a freeze Sat night and Sun \nnight as lows get into the 30s each night.",
